pkg.description:
C3 application package this Type System element _belongs_ to; note that this maybe different from the package
where this type-system element was declared.

@see #package

storedCalc.description:
Indicates that the field calculation should be persisted. This is more expensive on update, but allows calculated
values to be used in filter expressions.

@see #calcExpr

notPersistable.description:
If `true` then value of this field will _not_ be persisted in the database. This is either due to an explicit
`not persistable` suffix on the field declaration or because it is a (non-stored) calculated field.

@see #calcExpr
@see #storedCalc

final.description:
True if this field is declared final. This mans that it may not be changed by types that mix it in. Note that
the type itself may not be final, which implies all fields are final.

@see Type#final

constant.description:
True if this field is declared constant. That means its value can never be changed from the value specified
by its default.

@see #default

title:
A field type defines a single member of a {@link Type type}.
The two key elements are the field's *name* and *value type*.

Fields are not only data fields (member variables), such as primitives, collections and objects;
fields can also be methods, which represent functions callable on the type.
Methods are simply fields with a {@link MethodType method value type}.

Fields declared on a type are automatically included in any type that mixes in that type.
The sub-type can override the field declaration to a limited extent, generally only adding
annotations or narrowing the scope.

More common than overriding is using parametric references to define interfaces that
don't fully specify the data type. (See {@link NormTimeseries}).

```c3typ
type Point<C> {
  x: !C
  y: !C

  magnitude: abstract member function(): C
}

type IntegerPoint mixes Point<int>
```

In the example above, the value type of the x and y data fields in the Point type is an unbound parametric
reference to the variable "C" ({@link VarReferenceType}).  IntegerPoint binds this variable to integer primitive,
which makes the value type of x and y in the IntegerPoint type integer primitives ({@link PrimitiveType}).

```c3typ
type IntegerPoint {
  x: !int
  y: !int

  magnitude: abstract member function(): int
}
```

The implementation of IntegerPoint.magnitude in JavaScript might be:

```js
function magnitude() {
  return Math.round(Math.sqrt(this.x * this.x + this.y * this.y));
}
```

In documentation, fields that are inherited without being redeclared in the child type are
marked as "inherited" and grouped by the type they are declared on.

@see Type
@see ValueType
@see DeclaredFieldType
@see FieldType
